    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born in 1126 into a family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> judges, Averroes (Abū l-Walīd Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad Ibn Rushd) served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> as judge and physician and wrote commentaries on nearly all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>'s surviving works at a caliph's request.</li><li>In the Decisive Treatise he argued that philosophy cannot contradict revelation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, since both are methods of reaching the truth and &quot;truth cannot contradict truth&quot;, and he answered al-Ghazali's attack on philosophy in the Incoherence of the Incoherence.</li><li>A tribunal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> condemned his teachings and banished him in 1195, but his commentaries, translated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, reached Jewish and Christian scholars, while his influence in the Islamic world remained modest until modern times.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Averroes wrote commentaries on nearly all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>'s surviving works; the exception was the Politics, which he could not obtain, so he wrote on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-republic">Plato's Republic</a> instead.</p></aside>
    </section>

