    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1126</span><span class="rf-text">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> on 14 April, the year his grandfather, the city's chief judge, died</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1153</span><span class="rf-text">In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>, capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a>, makes astronomical observations and supports the building of new colleges</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1169</span><span class="rf-text">Introduced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-tufayl">Ibn Tufayl</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Abu Yaqub Yusuf, begins his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> commentaries and is appointed qadi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1178</span><span class="rf-text">Writes the Decisive Treatise on the compatibility of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and philosophy</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1182</span><span class="rf-text">Succeeds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-tufayl">Ibn Tufayl</a> as court physician and is appointed chief qadi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1195</span><span class="rf-text">Tried by a tribunal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, which condemns his teachings, orders his works burned and banishes him to Lucena</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1198</span><span class="rf-text">Dies on 11 December, shortly after returning to court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1270</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> condemns 15 doctrines, many of them Aristotelian or Averroist</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Averroes, known in Arabic as Ibn Rushd, was born Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Rushd on 14 April 1126 (520 AH) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. Little is known of his early life. His family was well known in the city for public service, especially in legal and religious fields. His grandfather Abu al-Walid Muhammad (d. 1126) was chief judge (qadi) of Córdoba and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> of its Great Mosque under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a>, and his father Abu al-Qasim Ahmad was also chief judge until the Almoravids were replaced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> in 1146. A nickname, al-Hafid ("the Grandson"), distinguishes him from his grandfather.</p><p>According to his traditional biographers, his education was "excellent". It began with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a>, jurisprudence, medicine and theology. He learned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> jurisprudence under al-Hafiz Abu Muhammad ibn Rizq and hadith with Ibn Bashkuwal, a student of his grandfather, and his father taught him jurisprudence, including Imam Malik's Muwatta, which he memorised. He studied medicine under Abu Jafar Jarim al-Tajail, who probably taught him philosophy too, and he knew the works of Ibn Bajjah (Avempace).</p><p>He also studied the kalam theology of the Ash'ari school, which he criticised later in life. His 13th-century biographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-al-abbar">Ibn al-Abbar</a> said he was more interested in law and its principles than in hadith and was especially competent in khilaf, the disputes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-jurisprudence">Islamic jurisprudence</a>. Ibn al-Abbar also mentioned his interest in "the sciences of the ancients", probably Greek philosophy and science.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Averroes&#8221; cites where it covers Early life and education:</p><ul><li>Roger Arnaldez, Ibn Rushd (Brill and Luzac &amp; co., 1986)</li><li>Avital Wohlman, Al-Ghazali, Averroes and the Interpretation of the Qur'an: Common Sense and Philosophy in Islam (Routledge, 2009)</li><li>Albert Z. Iskandar, Ibn Rushd (Averroës) (Springer, 2008)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/plato"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plato.jpg" alt="Plato" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plato_Silanion_Musei_Capitolini_MC1377.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marie-Lan Nguyen, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Averroes joined a regular meeting of philosophers, physicians and poets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, attended by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-tufayl">Ibn Tufayl</a>, Ibn Zuhr and the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Abu Yusuf Yaqub. By 1153 he was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>, capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a>, to make astronomical observations and to support the Almohad project of building new colleges. He hoped to find physical laws of astronomical movements and not only the mathematical laws then known, but this research was unsuccessful. He likely met Ibn Tufayl, author of Hayy ibn Yaqdhan and court physician, and the two became friends despite differences in their philosophies.</p><p>In 1169 Ibn Tufayl introduced him to the Almohad caliph Abu Yaqub Yusuf. In an account reported by the historian 'Abd al-Wahid al-Marrakushi, the caliph asked whether the heavens had existed since eternity or had a beginning. Averroes, knowing the question was controversial and fearing danger, did not answer. The caliph set out the views of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> and Muslim philosophers and discussed them with Ibn Tufayl, which put Averroes at ease, and he then gave his own view. He later said the caliph had "a profuseness of learning I did not suspect".</p><p>He remained in the caliph's favour until the caliph died in 1184. When the caliph complained to Ibn Tufayl that Aristotle was hard to understand, Ibn Tufayl recommended Averroes to explain him. His first works on the subject date from 1169, and in the same year he was appointed qadi of Seville.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Averroes&#8221; cites where it covers Career:</p><ul><li>Roger Arnaldez, Ibn Rushd (Brill and Luzac &amp; co., 1986)</li><li>Albert Z. Iskandar, Ibn Rushd (Averroës) (Springer, 2008)</li><li>Avital Wohlman, Al-Ghazali, Averroes and the Interpretation of the Qur'an: Common Sense and Philosophy in Islam (Routledge, 2009)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristotle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aristotle.jpg" alt="Aristotle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aristotle_Altemps_Inv8575.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">After Lysippos, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As qadi he decided cases and gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwas</a> based on religious law. He became qadi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in 1171 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> again in 1179, and in 1182 succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-tufayl">Ibn Tufayl</a> as court physician; later that year he was appointed chief qadi of Córdoba, an office his grandfather had held. His writing rate rose despite these duties and his travels in the Almohad empire. In law he mostly followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a>, dominant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, but sometimes rejected the argument of the "consensus of the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>". His Bidāyat al-Mujtahid explains the differences between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> schools of law and why they are inevitable.</p><p>He wrote commentaries on nearly all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>'s surviving works, in three kinds: short, middle and long. Only the Physics, Metaphysics, On the Soul, On the Heavens and Posterior Analytics had all three. Among his own treatises, the Decisive Treatise (1178) argues that revelation and philosophy are two methods of reaching the truth, so that scripture which seems to contradict reason must be interpreted allegorically by those "rooted in knowledge". In 1179 he criticised the Ash'arites in the Exposition of the Methods of Proof, and in 1180 he answered al-Ghazali's Incoherence of the Philosophers in the Incoherence of the Incoherence.</p><p>In medicine he wrote the Kulliyat (Colliget) around 1162, and described stroke as produced by the brain and the signs of what resembles Parkinson's disease.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Averroes&#8221; cites where it covers Works, Commentaries on Aristotle, Stand-alone philosophical works, Islamic theology:</p><ul><li>Peter Adamson, Philosophy in the Islamic World: A History of Philosophy Without Any Gaps (Oxford University Press, 2016)</li><li>Roger Arnaldez, Ibn Rushd (Brill and Luzac &amp; co., 1986)</li><li>Nidhal Guessoum, Islam's Quantum Question: Reconciling Muslim Tradition and Modern Science (I.B. Tauris, 2011)</li><li>Richard C. Taylor, Averroes: religious dialectic and Aristotelian philosophical thought (Cambridge University Press, 2005)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/the-republic"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-republic.jpg" alt="The Republic" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/the-republic">The Republic</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Politeia_beginning._Codex_Parisinus_graecus_1807.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Plato, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Averroes criticised his predecessors. He faulted al-Farabi for merging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> and for misreading Aristotelian logic, and he argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Avicenna</a>'s theory of emanation had fallacies and was not found in Aristotle. He answered al-Ghazali, who had charged the philosophers with unbelief, by saying that many of his criticisms applied only to Avicenna and not to Aristotle.</p><p>In 1195 his fortune reversed. Various charges were made against him, and a tribunal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> condemned his teachings, ordered his works burned and banished him to nearby Lucena. Early biographers pointed to a possible insult to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in his writings. The Encyclopaedia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> said the caliph distanced himself from Averroes to win the support of more orthodox ulema, whom he needed for his war against Christian kingdoms, and Majid Fakhry wrote that pressure from traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> jurists played a part.</p><p>Abbas Mahmoud al-Aqqad argued that the causes were political and personal. He noted that Averroes lacked diplomatic tact and had called the caliph the "King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a>" in a commentary on zoology, and that his friendship with the caliph's brother Abu Yahya, governor of Córdoba, aroused suspicion in wartime. Mohammed Abed al-Jabri held that he was punished for condemning tyranny in his commentary on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-republic">Plato's Republic</a>. The campaign was wider than Averroes: the authorities also burned foundational Maliki texts such as the Mudawwana of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahnun">Sahnun</a> and banned the study of applied jurisprudence.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-arabi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibn-arabi.jpg" alt="Ibn Arabi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-arabi">Ibn Arabi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%82%D8%A8%D8%B1_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%B4%D9%8A%D8%AE_%D9%85%D8%AD%D9%8A%D9%8A_%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%AF%D9%8A%D9%86_%D8%A8%D9%86_%D8%B9%D8%B1%D8%A8%D9%8A.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Omar.Hafez, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Al-Aqqad held that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> pardoned Averroes and restored him to favour as soon as the political tensions eased. After a few years Averroes returned to court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> and was again in the caliph's favour. The caliph was Abu Yusuf Yaqub, who had succeeded Abu Yaqub Yusuf in 1184.</p><p>Averroes died shortly afterwards, on 11 December 1198 (9 Safar 595 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-calendar">Islamic calendar</a>). He was first buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. His body was later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> for another funeral, which the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> mystic and philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-arabi">Ibn Arabi</a> (1165–1240) attended.</p><p>In his astronomy, Averroes had criticised the Ptolemaic system, rejecting eccentrics and epicycles, and had tried to reconcile astronomy with the physics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>. Later in life he declared that this attempt had failed and said he had neither the time nor the knowledge to reconcile the observed planetary motions with Aristotelian principles. He also said of the astronomy of his time that it "offers no truth, but only agrees with the calculations and not with what exists." The astronomer Nur ad-Din al-Bitruji (d. 1204) adopted most of his reform principles and proposed an early astronomical system based on Aristotelian physics.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas.jpg" alt="Thomas Aquinas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St-thomas-aquinasFXD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carlo Crivelli, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>His legacy in the Islamic world was modest. Part of the reason was geography, as he lived in the far west of Islamic civilisation, and his focus on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> was outdated in the 12th-century Muslim world, which had scrutinised Aristotle since the ninth century and was engaging with newer schools, especially that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Avicenna</a>. In the 19th century, during the Al-Nahda, Muslim thinkers began to engage with his works again as an inspiration to modernise Muslim intellectual tradition.</p><p>Jewish scholars received him eagerly. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maimonides">Maimonides</a> (d. 1204) said Averroes "was extremely right". Joseph Ibn Kaspi translated his commentaries on Aristotle's Organon in 1232, and in 1260 Moses ibn Tibbon published translations of almost all his commentaries and some medical works. Jewish Averroism peaked in the 14th century.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Christendom</a> Michael Scot began translating the long commentaries in 1217, and Averroes became known simply as "The Commentator". The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Averroists, among them Siger of Brabant and Boethius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>. In 1270 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Paris, Étienne Tempier, condemned 15 doctrines, and in 1277 he condemned 219 theses drawn mainly from Aristotle and Averroes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a> relied on Averroes's reading of Aristotle but attacked his theory that all humans share one intellect. Averroism kept a following until the 16th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> placed him in the first circle of hell, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a> painted him in The School of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Averroes&#8221; cites where it covers In Jewish tradition, In Latin tradition, In Islamic tradition:</p><ul><li>Peter Adamson, Philosophy in the Islamic World: A History of Philosophy Without Any Gaps (Oxford University Press, 2016)</li><li>Olivier Leaman, An Introduction to Classical Islamic Philosophy (Cambridge University Press, 2002)</li><li>Antoni Bordoy Fernandez, Ramón Llull y la crítica al averroísmo cristiano (Universitat de les Illes Balears, 2002)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Averroes (Ibn Rushd, 1126–1198) was a judge and physician of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> who served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> and wrote commentaries on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>. He argued that philosophy and revelation agree, was briefly banished in 1195, and through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> translations shaped Jewish and Christian thought.</em></p>

